# WMS Module — Step-by-Step Build Prompts

This file breaks the full WMS specification into ten ordered prompts so you can build the module one piece at a time. Build them in order, because each step depends on the ones before it.

## How to use this

Keep the full WMS specification and the "Shared Context" block below available in your conversation with the AI coding assistant. Then paste one step prompt at a time. Before you start, tell the assistant your stack (for example: React, Vue, Angular, or plain JavaScript) and any libraries you already use in the barcode app, because the assistant should match your existing project rather than introduce a new framework. After each step, test that its "Done when" criteria are met before moving to the next step.

---

## Shared Context (keep this in the conversation for every step)

You are adding a self-contained Warehouse Management System (WMS) module to an existing barcode application. The module is fully dynamic, so the user designs the entire warehouse through the interface with nothing hardcoded. Everything is handled in the frontend, and all data is saved through a single storage adapter that uses browser storage now (IndexedDB, with localStorage as a fallback) and can later be switched to a backend API by changing only that adapter. The whole feature, and individual zones and locations, can be enabled or disabled. All scan workflows must work well on phones and handheld scanners with large touch targets and minimal typing. Match the existing barcode app's framework, language, and styling rather than introducing anything new. The full WMS specification is the source of truth; follow it for field names and rules.

---

## The build sequence at a glance

1. Module foundation — data models, storage adapter, state store.
2. WMS settings and the master enable/disable toggle.
3. Warehouse designer (the layout builder).
4. Block property editor.
5. Visual warehouse map and occupancy engine.
6. Validation engine and the barcode transfer/move flow.
7. Receiving and putaway, with pallet and box capture.
8. Taking a pallet out with the mandatory audit, plus picking.
9. Cycle count, search, filters, and reports.
10. Roles and permissions, label printing, API adapter, and polish.

---

## Step 1 — Module Foundation: data models, storage adapter, state store

Goal: stand up the data layer that everything else relies on.

Build: create the wms module folder and define every record described in the specification — warehouse, zone, location or block, the warehouse-relevant extension to the existing material or item record, pallet or license plate, inventory record, movement and audit log entry, and the WMS settings record — using whatever data-structure style fits the project. Build the pluggable storage adapter as a single layer that exposes list, get by identifier, create, update, and delete for every record type, with three interchangeable implementations: one using IndexedDB as the default, one using localStorage as a fallback, and one stub that will later call a backend API. Wire up the central state store, and make sure every screen will talk only to the adapter, never directly to storage. Keep occupancy, status colour, and utilization as values that will be computed later, not stored.

Spec reference: sections 1, 2, and 9.

Depends on: nothing.

Done when: all record types are defined, the adapter saves and reads data that survives a page reload, and the active adapter can be changed in one place.

---

## Step 2 — WMS Settings and the Master Toggle

Goal: control how the whole module behaves, and let it be turned off cleanly.

Build: create the settings panel backed by the settings record. Include the master flag that turns the whole WMS module on or off, the flag that forces location selection during transfers, the putaway mode (directed, manual, or hybrid), the pick strategy (first-in-first-out, last-in-first-out, or first-expired-first-out), the flag that makes outbound pallet audits mandatory, the flag that decides whether capacity violations hard-block or only warn, the flag that decides whether material-rule violations hard-block or only warn, the negative-stock flag, and the storage-adapter selector. When the master flag is off, the app must behave exactly as it does today, with no warehouse steps anywhere.

Spec reference: sections 2.8 and 6.6.

Depends on: Step 1.

Done when: settings save and load, and toggling the master flag visibly enables or disables the WMS feature across the app.

---

## Step 3 — Warehouse Designer (Layout Builder)

Goal: let the user design and save a warehouse through the interface.

Build: a visual designer that generates a grid from a chosen number of columns and rows, with optional vertical levels, and a chosen naming scheme (letters, numbers, custom prefix, and separator), creating all the locations in one action. Let the user add, rename, and delete individual columns, rows, levels, or locations afterward. Let the user optionally group a selected range of cells into a zone that carries a temperature classification, a type, and a colour. Provide bulk selection so the same change can be applied to many locations at once. Add the ability to save a layout as a reusable template and to clone a warehouse, to import a layout from a spreadsheet or structured file and export it for backup, and to undo and redo, with a live preview of the grid as it is built.

Spec reference: section 3.

Depends on: Steps 1 and 2.

Done when: a user can build a warehouse of any size with optional levels and zones, save it, reload the page, and see it persist.

