## Step 6 — Validation Engine and Barcode Transfer/Move Flow

Goal: move stock between blocks safely, with one shared set of rules.

Build: first, a single self-contained validation module that takes the item, the destination location, the quantity, and the settings and returns whether the action is allowed along with specific errors and warnings, covering every rule in the specification — feature on, location usable, reservation match, all four capacity dimensions, allowed and restricted material types, mixing, single lot, temperature, hazardous materials, expiry order, sufficient stock on the way out, and existence of any specified lot or serial. Then the transfer or move flow that uses it: the operator scans the source location or scans the item or pallet to locate it, scans the item or pallet and confirms the quantity or box count including lot if tracked, scans the destination location, the system validates, and on confirmation it decrements the source, increments the destination, updates the pallet's current location, writes a transfer entry to the movement log, and refreshes the map. Integrate with the existing scan interface, and make every rejection message specific.

Spec reference: sections 6.2, 6.6, and 7.

Depends on: Steps 1, 2, and 5.

Done when: a validated transfer updates inventory and the map, the log records it, and invalid moves are blocked with a clear reason.

---

## Step 7 — Receiving and Putaway, with pallet and box capture

Goal: get stock into the warehouse and into the right blocks.

Build: the receiving and putaway flow. The operator scans the item or the incoming pallet license plate, which identifies the material, and for a new pallet records the number of boxes on the pallet, the units per box if applicable, and any lot and expiry. If putaway is directed or hybrid, the system suggests the best available block based on allowed material type, available capacity, zone and temperature match, and pick optimization; in manual mode the operator chooses. The operator scans the destination location, the system validates using the engine from Step 6, and on confirmation it creates or updates the inventory and pallet records, writes a putaway entry to the movement log, and refreshes the map.

Spec reference: sections 2.5, 2.6, and 6.1.

Depends on: Steps 1, 5, and 6.

Done when: stock can be received onto a pallet with a box count and put away into a validated block, and it then appears on the map.

---

## Step 8 — Taking a Pallet Out (mandatory audit) and Picking

Goal: the required outbound audit, plus order picking.

Build: the take-a-pallet-out flow with a mandatory audit step performed before anything is removed. The operator scans the pallet license plate, or scans the location and selects the pallet from the list in that block. The system then shows an audit and verification screen that prominently displays the item name and the number of boxes present on the pallet, and also shows the stock-keeping unit, the lot or batch number, the expiry date, the current location, and the total unit quantity. The operator physically checks the pallet and confirms whether it matches; this confirmation is the audit. On a confirmed match, the system removes or decrements the inventory, updates the pallet status, writes the outbound entry to the movement log, and refreshes the map. On a reported discrepancy, the operator enters the corrected box count, the system flags it, posts an adjustment, and records the audit and correction in the log, with the option to require supervisor approval on large discrepancies. The action only completes after the audit is confirmed, and when the mandatory-audit setting is on the screen cannot be skipped. Then build picking: load a pick list or scan an order, direct the operator to locations in pick-sequence order and by the chosen strategy, scan to confirm the location and item, enter the quantity, and apply the same outbound audit when a full pallet is picked.

Spec reference: sections 6.3 and 6.4.

Depends on: Steps 1, 6, and 7.

Done when: a pallet cannot leave without passing the audit screen showing item name and box count, discrepancies are logged as adjustments, and picking is directed and confirmed by scanning.

---

## Step 9 — Cycle Count, Search, Filters, and Reports

Goal: operational visibility and stock accuracy.

Build: the cycle count and audit flow, where the operator selects a location or zone, scans items and pallets and enters counted quantities and box counts, the system compares them to the recorded figures, flags discrepancies, and lets the operator post a logged adjustment. Then the reporting set: find an item by answering where a given item or lot is, with quantities and highlighted locations; find empty or available space that can accept a given material type and quantity; a utilization report by warehouse, zone, and location with empty-versus-full counts; stock-by-location and location-by-stock reports; a movement and audit history filtered by date, user, type, item, location, or pallet, including all outbound audits and discrepancies; a replenishment list of locations below their minimum stock; and an expiry report of stock approaching its expiry date.

Spec reference: sections 6.5 and 8.

Depends on: Steps 1, 7, and 8.

Done when: counts post adjustments correctly and every report returns accurate results.

---

## Step 10 — Roles and Permissions, Label Printing, API Adapter, and Polish

Goal: make it production-ready.

Build: at least two roles, where an administrator or manager can design the warehouse, edit properties, change settings, enable or disable features, and approve large audit discrepancies, while an operator or picker can only run the scan workflows; gate the designer and settings screens behind the administrator role. Add a print-and-export sheet for all location barcodes and all pallet license-plate labels. Implement the API storage adapter and confirm the whole module works when it is selected, with no changes needed to the interface or the logic. Finish the user-experience polish: large thumb-friendly controls, success and failure feedback using colour together with sound or vibration, specific validation and audit messages, a readable mobile fallback for the map, and a confirmation before any destructive action.

Spec reference: sections 9, 10, and 11.

Depends on: all previous steps.

Done when: roles restrict access correctly, labels print, the module runs on the API adapter, and the workflows feel solid on a phone.

